<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Göppingen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Göppingen is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, on the River Fils east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>. Lying below the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> hill, the ancestral seat of the Staufer emperors, it became an industrial town in the nineteenth century, and the toy and model railway maker <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marklin">Märklin</a>, founded there in 1859, still has its headquarters in the town.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/goppingen.jpg" alt="Göppingen" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stadt Faurndau · CC BY-SA 2.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Faurndau_Stiftskirche.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Göppingen is part of the Stuttgart Region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a> and the capital of the district of Göppingen. It lies at the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> mountain, in the valley of the river Fils. Its districts are Bartenbach, Bezgenriet, Faurndau, Hohenstaufen, Holzheim, Jebenhausen and Maitis, besides Göppingen itself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Tradition holds that the town was founded by an Alemannic leader called Geppo in the 3rd or 4th century. A fire on 25 August 1782 destroyed most of it, and it was rebuilt at once. Industrialisation in the 19th century made the area a centre of industry, and companies such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marklin">Märklin</a> and Schuler show its importance still.</p><p>Göppingen and the nearby village of Jebenhausen had a Jewish community from 1777 until the Holocaust. In that year the Baron von Liebenstein issued a letter of protection allowing Elias Gutmann and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> to settle in Jebenhausen. The community peaked in the middle of the 19th century, when its centre shifted from Jebenhausen to Göppingen, and many families emigrated to America by the late 19th century. The synagogue built in 1881 was destroyed in the Kristallnacht of 9 and 10 November 1938. Rabbi Aron Tänzer, who served the community from 1907 to 1937, collected detailed records and published a history of the Jews of Jebenhausen and Göppingen in 1927. The Jewish Museum in Jebenhausen opened in 1992.</p><p>A civilian airfield built north of the town in 1930 was taken over by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luftwaffe">Luftwaffe</a> in 1935 and expanded into a barracks. From 1945 to 1949 it housed displaced persons and refugees. It was renamed Cooke Barracks in 1949, and from late 1950 United States Army units were stationed there, among them divisions that used it as headquarters. The barracks were returned to the German government in 1992 and later returned to civilian use.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Göppingen is the home of the toy company <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marklin">Märklin</a> and the headquarters of TeamViewer. The local handball club Frisch Auf Göppingen plays in the men's top division of the Handball-Bundesliga. The town has its own daily newspaper, the Neue Württembergische Zeitung, and its twin towns are Foggia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klosterneuburg">Klosterneuburg</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, Pessac in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and Sonneberg in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Among its natives are the mathematician and astronomer Michael Maestlin, the mentor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-kepler">Johannes Kepler</a>, and the pietist theologian Friedrich Christoph Oetinger.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Göppingen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In industry, the inventions of Daimler, Maybach and Bosch made Stuttgart one of the birthplaces of the motor car and its electrical equipment, firms that still bear their founders' names, while Zeppelin's airships, Margarete Steiff's toy animals at Giengen and Märklin's model trains at <b>Göppingen</b> became widely known.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wurttemberg">Kingdom of Württemberg · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
